WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.340
A plant or animal cell keeps its chromosomes inside the nucleus.

2
00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:08.360
How can their instructions reach a ribosome outside?

3
00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:14.420
Watch the blue DNA row remain behind while a green copy begins to grow.

4
00:00:17.883 --> 00:00:20.943
Making a protein from a gene is protein synthesis.

5
00:00:22.343 --> 00:00:23.863
The first stage is transcription.

6
00:00:25.263 --> 00:00:27.643
An enzyme opens a short stretch of DNA.

7
00:00:28.983 --> 00:00:31.923
It uses the template strand to build messenger RNA.

8
00:00:33.463 --> 00:00:35.303
Only the selected gene is copied.

9
00:00:36.433 --> 00:00:39.583
Each green RNA block pairs with a blue DNA block.

10
00:00:39.583 --> 00:00:49.553
DNA letter A pairs with RNA letter U. T pairs with A. C pairs with G, and G pairs

11
00:00:49.553 --> 00:00:53.193
with C. RNA uses U instead of T.

12
00:00:54.217 --> 00:00:55.917
Try the next template group.

13
00:00:57.217 --> 00:00:58.997
The blue DNA blocks read TAC.

14
00:01:00.297 --> 00:01:03.517
Which green messenger RNA blocks will pair with them?

15
00:01:04.717 --> 00:01:07.357
Keep the pairing key in view and choose the full group.

16
00:01:13.333 --> 00:01:22.273
T pairs with A. A pairs with U. C pairs with G. The messenger group is AUG.

17
00:01:23.433 --> 00:01:25.853
The enzyme releases the finished message.

18
00:01:27.073 --> 00:01:31.613
The green strand peels away, while the blue DNA closes and stays inside.

19
00:01:32.733 --> 00:01:36.853
The messenger RNA passes through a pore into the cytoplasm.

20
00:01:38.273 --> 00:01:41.853
Translation begins when a gold ribosome grips the message.

21
00:01:43.113 --> 00:01:44.713
It reads each codon in order.

22
00:01:45.973 --> 00:01:50.153
A codon is a group of three letters that means an amino acid or stop.

23
00:01:51.217 --> 00:01:56.077
Purple transfer RNA brings the amino acid matched to each codon.

24
00:01:57.217 --> 00:02:00.797
Its anticodon pairs with the codon inside the ribosome.

25
00:02:01.937 --> 00:02:05.877
The ribosome joins each red amino acid to the growing chain.

26
00:02:07.157 --> 00:02:11.397
At a stop codon, no amino acid is added and the chain is released.

27
00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:15.760
Reading usually begins at the start codon AUG.

28
00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:20.220
It adds methionine and fixes the grouping that follows.

29
00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:24.120
The ribosome then moves codon by codon.

30
00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:30.180
At UGA, the stop sign appears, the reading window opens, and the finished chain

31
00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:31.180
floats free.

32
00:02:31.883 --> 00:02:35.003
The released chain can fold into a working protein.

33
00:02:36.163 --> 00:02:39.903
The message changed form, but its order guided every handoff.

34
00:02:41.183 --> 00:02:47.443
In protein-coding genes, DNA order sets mRNA codons and amino acid order.

35
00:02:48.563 --> 00:02:52.663
That is how instructions inside the nucleus reach a protein outside.
